<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TRadioButton</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TRadioButton" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TRadioButton</tt> extends <a href="?page=Controls.CheckBox">TCheckBox</a> and displays
a radio button on a Web page. It inherits the caption, layout, attribute, and validation
properties of a check box, and it raises the same <tt>OnCheckedChanged</tt> event. It adds
grouping, so that at most one button in a group is selected.
</p>

<h2>Grouping</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>GroupName</tt> names the group a button belongs to. A browser allows one selection
among the input elements that share a <tt>name</tt> attribute, so the group is what the
control writes into that attribute.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TRadioButton GroupName="size" Text="Small" /&gt;
&lt;com:TRadioButton GroupName="size" Text="Medium" /&gt;
&lt;com:TRadioButton GroupName="size" Text="Large" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The rendered name is not <tt>GroupName</tt> by itself. The control prefixes it with the
path of its naming container, and <tt>UniqueGroupName</tt> returns the result. Two
repeater items that each hold a <tt>size</tt> group therefore render two separate groups,
and a selection in one item leaves the other item alone.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Condition</th><th>Rendered name</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>GroupName</tt> is set</td>
	<td>The naming container path followed by <tt>GroupName</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>GroupName</tt> is empty</td>
	<td>The unique id of the button, so the button stands alone in its own group.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>The button sits in a <tt>TRadioButtonList</tt></td>
	<td>The unique id of the list, so the list forms one group.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>UniqueGroupName</tt> is set</td>
	<td>That value, with no prefix.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
Setting <tt>UniqueGroupName</tt> directly overrides the whole calculation. That is how
buttons in different naming containers join one group, which <tt>GroupName</tt> cannot
express.
</p>

<h2>Reading a group</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>RadioButtonsInGroup</tt> returns an array of the buttons that share this button's
rendered group name. Only buttons present in the current page hierarchy appear in it. A
handler uses the array to find which button the user chose.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
foreach ($this-&gt;Small-&gt;RadioButtonsInGroup as $button) {
    if ($button-&gt;Checked) {
        $this-&gt;Result-&gt;Text = $button-&gt;Text;
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>The posted value</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Every button in a group posts the same name, so the value attribute is what tells them
apart. <tt>Value</tt> sets it. A button that leaves <tt>Value</tt> empty posts its own
unique id instead, which keeps the buttons distinct without any configuration.
</p>

